[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186162110 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-af95ddae-1d2c-5a3d-9f8a-8a341d008cae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162378 :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}∼N(5,{10}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $50$ from a normal population, calculate $P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\n then the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(5,\\frac{{10}^{2}}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(5,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2) & =P(\\frac{4.8-5}{\\sqrt{2}}<Z<\\frac{5.2-5}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.14<Z<0.14) \\\\  & =Φ(0.14)-Φ(-0.14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.14)=0.5557,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,Φ(-0.14) & =1-Φ(0.14) \\\\  & =1-0.5557 \\\\  & =0.4443.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4.8<\\overset{―}{X}<5.2) & =Φ(0.14)-Φ(-0.14) \\\\  & =0.5557-0.4443 \\\\  & =0.1114.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159470 :answer-field/correct 17592186159473 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-af95ddae-1d2c-5a3d-9f8a-8a341d008cae" :question/id #uuid "af95ddae-1d2c-5a3d-9f8a-8a341d008cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-143298" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{18}∼N(2,{6}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $18$ from a normal population, calculate $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05).$\n\n*Hint: Value tables of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2578\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2912\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3192\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0119\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0116\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0113\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0154\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0150\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0146\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0197\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0192\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0188\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(2,\\frac{{6}^{2}}{18}) \\\\  & ∼N(2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05) & =P(-1.05<\\overset{―}{X}<1.05) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{-1.05-2}{\\sqrt{2}}<Z<\\frac{1.05-2}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =P(-2.16<Z<-0.67) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.67)-Φ(-2.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.67) & =0.2514, \\\\ Φ(-2.16) & =0.0154.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<1.05) & =Φ(-0.67)-Φ(-2.16) \\\\  & =0.2514-0.0154 \\\\  & =0.2360.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/diffic